+## Damaged and incomplete tails
+
+A frame that is cut short at the end of the file, or a last frame whose checksum or payload is bad, is what an interrupted write leaves. It was never acknowledged, so a writer cuts it off (under the queue lock, before writing anything else) and counts it in `tornTailsDropped`. A bad frame with more bytes after it, or a bad magic or length, cannot be an interrupted write: it is damage, and raises `CorruptLogError` with the offset. The file is left as it was. A frame can be any size up to the 64 MiB limit; a larger record is refused before anything is written.
 
 Job ids are `j<n>`, where `n` is the count of jobs ever enqueued, and are never reused.
